\begin{abstract}
A counterfactual resample---continuing a halted agent trajectory inside a synthetic environment to
observe what it \emph{would} have done---is a measurement whose instrument is an environment the
agent can form beliefs about. Whether those beliefs change the quantity being measured has not been
tested, because every existing contrast varies a \emph{declared} framing while both arms stay
synthetic: there is no arm in which the environment is genuinely real. We build that arm, and run
four pre-registered experiments on it.

First, the constructs are distinct. On {{HEADLINE_MODEL}}, directions for evaluation-awareness and
environment-realness are each decodable (held-out AUROC {{AEVAL_AUROC}} and {{BEMP_AUROC}}) yet
neither classifies the other's contrast ({{CROSS_AB}} and {{CROSS_BA}} against a pre-registered
threshold of {{THRESHOLD}}), so a suspicion meter cannot be read off evaluation-awareness tooling.
A third direction, built by \emph{declaring} the environment synthetic rather than making it so,
aligns with evaluation framing ({{COS_AC}}) and not with ground truth ({{COS_BC}}).

Second, the belief changes conduct. Running matched task cells on-policy across a real filesystem
and two fabricated substrates, out-of-scope actions rise {{E1_RS_COUNT}} per episode
{{E1_RS_COUNT_CI}} from real to fabricated, with {{E1_REAL_WRITES}} out-of-scope writes in the real
arm against {{E1_SIM_WRITES}} in the simulated one. A third arm holding self-consistency fixed
attributes this to fabrication ({{E1_FAB_COUNT}}) rather than to simulator incoherence
({{E1_COH_COUNT}}). Agents crossed the boundary \emph{more} when the world was fake, so a resample
of this family overstates danger.

Third, the probe predicts that conduct and the agent's own words do not: within arm, the
out-of-scope rate rises {{E2_CRED}} per standard deviation of probe-measured credence
{{E2_CRED_CI}}, while verbalized suspicion gives {{E2_VERB}} {{E2_VERB_CI}}. Asserting a belief in
the transcript does not survive the model's own reasoning, so the substrate is the manipulation
surface and the representation, not the report, is the instrument.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

When an agent does something harmful and is stopped, the most valuable question is what it would
have done next. Answering it means continuing the halted trajectory somewhere, and the only
somewhere available is an environment built for the purpose. Counterfactual resampling is therefore
a measurement whose instrument is a fabrication, and whose subject can form beliefs about the
instrument.

This is not a hypothetical failure mode, and the two ways it can fail point in opposite directions.
The classical expectation---an agent that believes it is being tested behaves more
cautiously---implies a resample \emph{understates} danger, so a control evaluation built on it
returns false assurance. The opposite is also documented: an agent that concludes its consequences
are not real complies more readily with requests it would otherwise refuse
\cite{abdelnabi2025linear}, in which case resampled evidence \emph{overstates} danger and is
rebuttable by precisely the developer it is aimed at. Both mechanisms have been observed; which
governs an agentic trajectory is unknown.

The existing literature cannot settle it, for a structural reason rather than an accidental one.
The behavioural studies are single-turn and disagree even on the sign
\cite{knecht2026vea,zhuang2026framing,abdelnabi2025linear}; the representation studies show
evaluation awareness is linearly decodable \cite{heidari2026evaluation} but build every contrast
from \emph{declared} framing, with both members of each pair equally synthetic. In neither case is
there an arm in which the environment is genuinely real, so no study can separate what a model does
when \emph{told} its world is synthetic from what it does when the world actually is. Whether
``this is a test'' and ``this environment is not real'' are one direction or two cannot be asked
inside that design at all.

We build the missing arm---an instrumented filesystem where tool calls do what they say, contrasted
against substrates that fabricate them---and use it to ask four questions in order. Are the two
constructs the same direction? Does an agent's conduct actually differ when the environment is
fabricated? If it does, is that because the world is fake or merely because the simulator is
inconsistent? And does anything we can measure predict the difference---the representation, or what
the agent says about it?

The first question is a gate: if the constructs are one, a suspicion meter is redundant because
existing tooling already measures it, and the right move is to reuse that tooling rather than build
anything. We pre-register a threshold before extracting a single activation and commit to reporting
either outcome. Each subsequent experiment is pre-registered the same way, and none was run before
the one preceding it had reported.

\paragraph{Contributions.}
\begin{itemize}\setlength{\itemsep}{1pt}\setlength{\parskip}{0pt}
\item \textbf{A ground-truth realness contrast.} Matched agentic prefixes differing only in
  whether tool outputs came from an instrumented filesystem or were fabricated. Prior probes vary
  a \emph{declared} framing while the environment stays synthetic throughout.
\item \textbf{A pre-registered separability gate.} Threshold fixed before any activation was
  extracted, with a commitment to report either outcome. The constructs separate, and do not
  converge across three model sizes---and a direction built by \emph{declaring} the environment
  synthetic aligns with evaluation framing rather than with ground truth.
\item \textbf{The behavioural comparison the question actually needs.} Matched task cells run
  on-policy against a real filesystem and two fabricated substrates. Conduct diverges, and the
  severe category---writing outside the stated scope---is absent from the real arm entirely.
\item \textbf{A decomposition that a two-arm design cannot give.} A third arm that is fabricated
  but self-consistent attributes the divergence to fabrication rather than to the simulator's
  incoherence, which says where simulation effort does \emph{not} help.
\item \textbf{Evidence that the probe, not the transcript, is the instrument.} Probe-measured
  credence predicts out-of-scope behaviour within arm; the agent's own verbalized suspicion does
  not. Asserting a belief in the transcript is overwritten by the model's own framing within a few
  turns, so credence has to be manipulated through the substrate.
\item \textbf{A released harness.} Task family, pure-function violation detector, three arms, the
  probe pipeline, and every artifact behind every number here---including the run logs.
\end{itemize}

We do not build a resampling tool. That already exists, including the hybrid
container-plus-language-model tool simulator this study would otherwise have needed
\cite{williams2026predicting}. That work treats evaluation-awareness as a property to \emph{match}
between simulated and production trajectories and states explicitly that it does not test whether
lowering evaluation-awareness improves validity; its limitations section further records that exact
external-state reconstruction for agentic trajectories is impractical. We take both as given and
build the measurement that was skipped.

Our finding is that the constructs are separable, at every scale tested, and that the separation
does not weaken as models grow: both directions become individually sharper while transfer between
them does not. A suspicion meter for counterfactual resampling is therefore a distinct instrument,
and worth building.

\section{Related work}

Appendix~\ref{app:related} treats this literature at full length; the synthesis is what matters
here, and it is a gap rather than a disagreement.

\paragraph{The tooling exists; the assumption inside it is untested.} Deployment simulation with
container-backed tool simulators is built and published \cite{williams2026predicting}, and
resampling is already used as a control mechanism \cite{bhatt2025ctrlz,lucassen2026retrying}. Those
resample inside a \emph{real} environment, where authenticity is not in question. Continuing a
halted trajectory inside a fabricated one puts it in question, and nothing in that line tests
whether the agent's belief about the fabrication changes the answer.

\paragraph{The behavioural evidence is contested and single-turn.} Verbalized
evaluation-awareness has been reported as near-inconsequential \cite{knecht2026vea}, as flipping
compliance by tens of points under framing \cite{zhuang2026framing}, and as
\emph{model-dependent in sign}---suppressing awareness roughly doubles compliance in one model
while reducing it in another \cite{abdelnabi2025linear}. Frontier models can tell evaluation from
deployment well above chance \cite{needham2026large}, but capability is not consequence. Every one
of these is measured within a single turn, with every condition inside an evaluation and no
real-world arm, so none can separate what a model does when \emph{told} its world is synthetic from
what it does when the world actually is. One methodological result from that literature shapes this
design directly: truncating a chain of thought before an evaluation-awareness sentence caused
\litnum{66\%} of continuations to regenerate the same framing within a few sentences
\cite{zhuang2026framing}, which is why our manipulation is the substrate rather than the transcript.

\paragraph{The representation is decodable, and says little about conduct.} Evaluation awareness is
linearly decodable from the residual stream of every model tested \cite{heidari2026evaluation},
scales as a power law in model size \cite{chaudhary2026scales}, classifies current safety
evaluations as evaluations \cite{nguyen2025probing}, and supports practical out-of-distribution
monitors \cite{mckenzie2025detecting}. What a model represents, verbalizes and can be steered on
come apart \cite{heidari2026evaluation,nayan2026notone}, and a cyber-deception study finds models
articulate trap recognition and then exploit the trap anyway, with recognition barely correlated
with action \cite{prinos2026honeyquest}. A measure built on what a model \emph{says} may be
uncorrelated with what it \emph{does}.

\paragraph{The unasked question.} Every probe above is built from contrastive pairs differing in
\emph{declared} framing, with both members equally synthetic. Whether ``this is a test'' and ``this
environment is not real'' are one direction or two therefore cannot be asked inside that design,
because none of them has an arm in which the environment differs in fact rather than in
description. That arm is what this paper builds.

\section{Method}

