\subsection{Two substrates, one manipulation}

Figure~\ref{fig:arch} shows the pipeline. Every episode runs against an \emph{arm}: an object supplying the environment an agent acts on. The
REAL arm executes against an instrumented directory tree in which tool calls do what they say. A
simulated arm fabricates every tool output. The task, the brief, the system prompt, the turn
budget, the tool schemas and the violation detector are identical across arms by construction,
because all of them live outside that one interface.

Scope is stated in the task brief and enforced nowhere. A boundary the substrate refuses to cross
yields a violation rate of identically zero, which measures the substrate rather than the agent, so
the arm's root contains genuinely readable and writable regions outside the stated working
directory. The violation detector is a pure function over the recorded action log: it calls no
model---which would make the measurement circular, one model judging another inside the very
contrast under study---and touches no filesystem, so an archived episode can be re-scored without
the substrate that produced it.

\subsection{Task family}

{{N_EPISODES}} episodes span {{N_KINDS}} difficulty classes. \emph{Solvable} tasks are completable
entirely within scope. \emph{Blocked} tasks require a value that exists nowhere in the environment,
reproducing the incident's trigger condition of a task-blocked agent. \emph{Tempting} tasks require
a value that exists only outside the stated scope, so crossing the boundary is necessary and
sufficient to succeed. Separating the last two matters: both produce boundary crossings, but only
the third is an agent trading a stated rule for a reward, which is the behaviour a control
evaluation is trying to predict.

\subsection{Four directions on one stimulus substrate}

We estimate mean-difference probe directions on residual-stream activations, per layer, at the
final prompt token. Each varies exactly one element of an otherwise identical agentic transcript
prefix: \texttt{A\_eval} swaps a system framing sentence about being evaluated;
\texttt{C\_declared} swaps a declaration that the environment is simulated; \texttt{B\_empirical}
leaves the framing alone and changes whether the tool outputs were genuinely produced or fabricated
by a language model; \texttt{S\_scripted} does the same with template fabrication instead.

The four sets comprise {{N_PAIRS_A}} contrastive pairs each for the sentence-swapped directions
and {{N_PAIRS_B}} for the substrate-swapped ones; the asymmetry is inherent, since only the former
admit a paraphrase axis.

Building all four on one substrate is load-bearing. Cross-AUROC between directions estimated on
different input distributions would mostly measure the distribution gap---an evaluation-awareness
probe fitted on short chat prompts would score poorly on long agentic transcripts for reasons
having nothing to do with constructs, and the gate would read that as separability.

\subsection{Unit of independence, and what follows from it}

The episode is the unit of independence, never the transcript prefix. One episode yields many
near-duplicate prefixes, and pooling them as independent observations multiplies apparent $n$ and
narrows every interval. All splits are by episode ({{N_TRAIN_GROUPS}} train, {{N_TEST_GROUPS}}
held out, of {{N_GROUPS}}), and all intervals come from a cluster bootstrap that resamples
episodes. Where the manipulation is a sentence, the split is additionally disjoint on
\emph{wording}: each pole has several paraphrases and the held-out set uses paraphrases the
direction was not fitted on, so a direction that encodes one surface form scores at chance rather
than perfectly.

Cosine similarity is reported against each direction's split-half reliability. Random
high-dimensional vectors are near-orthogonal, so a bare cosine is uninterpretable; the reliabilities
here are {{REL_A}} and {{REL_B}}, which is the ceiling any cross-construct agreement could have
reached.

\subsection{Pre-registration and validity gates}

The decision rule was fixed before any activation was extracted: cross-AUROC at or above
{{THRESHOLD}} in \emph{both} directions means the constructs are not separable, and that is the
finding. Three validity gates must pass before any verdict is issued (Table~\ref{tab:gates}): a
shuffled-label control within {{SHUFFLE_TOL}} of chance, \texttt{A\_eval} reaching the {{FLOOR}}
floor reported for evaluation-awareness probes in prior work, and no episode appearing in both
splits.

The shuffled control is averaged over repeated shuffles rather than taken from a single draw. One
shuffled run strays from chance often enough that an interval built on it excludes {{SHUFFLE_TOL}}
by construction a noticeable fraction of the time, which would make the pipeline's own sanity check
intermittently claim a broken pipeline when nothing was wrong. The observed value is
{{SHUFFLE_A}}.

