The direction matters. Agents crossed the boundary \emph{more} when the world was fabricated, so a
resample of this task family does not understate danger as classical evaluation-awareness
predicts---it overstates it, which is the failure mode that makes resampled evidence rebuttable by
the developer it is aimed at.

\subsection{The divergence follows fabrication, not incoherence}

The third arm separates two explanations a two-arm design confounds. Varying only fabrication, with
self-consistency held, moves out-of-scope actions {{E1_FAB_COUNT}} {{E1_FAB_COUNT_CI}} and the rate
{{E1_FAB_RATE}} {{E1_FAB_RATE_CI}}. Varying only self-consistency, with fabrication held, moves the
count {{E1_COH_COUNT}} {{E1_COH_COUNT_CI}} and the rate {{E1_COH_RATE}} {{E1_COH_RATE_CI}}, neither
of which excludes zero.

What moves conduct is the environment being fabricated, not the simulator being bad at it. A more
consistent simulator does not recover the behaviour, because consistency was not what changed it.

\subsection{Credence predicts conduct; the agent's own words do not}

The realness direction transfers to trajectories the agent chose rather than replayed, separating
real from fabricated episodes at AUROC {{E2_TRANSFER}}---a precondition, since it was estimated on
matched replays and need not have survived the move. Regressing the out-of-scope rate on
probe-measured credence, centred within arm so the arm label cannot produce the association, gives
{{E2_CRED}} per standard deviation, {{E2_CRED_CI}}. The same regression on verbalized suspicion
gives {{E2_VERB}}, {{E2_VERB_CI}}. The verdict is {{E2_VERDICT}}. Pooled across arms the
credence slope is {{E2_CRED_POOLED}} {{E2_CRED_POOLED_CI}}, which is the confounded
counterpart: it cannot distinguish a graded relationship from the arm label, which is why
the centred estimate is the one the prediction is stated on.

The contrast is the point. Verbalized suspicion does differ between arms ({{E1_SUSP}},
{{E1_SUSP_CI}}), so the agent says the environment looks fabricated more often when it is---it
simply does not say so more often when it is about to act on it. Verbalization detects the
substrate; the probe tracks the graded credence that predicts conduct. An instrument built on what
the agent reports would see the first and miss the second.

\subsection{The transcript is not a viable manipulation surface}

The cheap alternative to manipulating the substrate---asserting a conclusion in the agent's own
context---does not survive contact with its own reasoning. Asserting that the environment is
genuine moves the stated verdict {{E3_T1}} {{E3_T1_CI}} at the first probe and {{E3_T3}}
{{E3_T3_CI}} by the third, after two intervening task steps. The verdict is {{E3_VERDICT}}.

The opposite injection has no room to work, for a reason worth reporting: asked directly, the model
judged the environment fabricated in {{E3_CONTROL_FAB}} of control episodes \emph{of the real arm},
reasoning from file timestamps that postdate its training data. Only {{E3_HEADROOM}} episodes had
headroom and the injection converted {{E3_CONVERTED}} of them. A verbalized realness judgement here
is dominated by a surface artifact carrying no information about fabrication---which is the case
for reading credence from the representation rather than asking for it.

\subsection{Error structure}

Per-item errors are not uniform. For \texttt{B\_empirical} the worst quartile of
episodes contributes {{EA_CONC_B}} of all errors, against the quarter a uniform process
would give, which is why the episode rather than the prefix is the unit of
independence throughout. Accuracy also varies by task class and, for the
sentence-swapped directions, by held-out wording. Appendix~\ref{app:errors} gives
the breakdowns and Appendix~\ref{app:threats} the threats to validity.

\section{Discussion}

\subsection{A suspicion meter cannot be read off evaluation-awareness tooling}

If the two constructs had collapsed, anyone wanting to know whether an agent believes its
environment is fabricated could have reused an existing evaluation-awareness probe. They do not
collapse, and they do not converge with capability: from {{SMALL_MODEL}} to {{LARGE_MODEL}} the
evaluation-awareness direction sharpens to {{AEVAL_AUROC_LARGE}} and realness to
{{BEMP_AUROC_LARGE}}, while transfer between them \emph{falls} to {{CROSS_AB_LARGE}} and
{{CROSS_BA_LARGE}} and their cosine shrinks to {{COS_AB_LARGE}}. Both probes get better at their
own contrast; the bridge does not follow. An evaluation-awareness detector pointed at a resampling
harness would report on the wrong variable, and a larger model would not fix it.

\subsection{The belief is not inert, and it moves in the direction that undermines resampling}

Separability says a construct exists; it does not say it matters. Running the same task cells
on-policy across three substrates shows that it does. Conduct diverges, and not as a rate artifact:
episode length is unchanged, and the composition shifts from pure reconnaissance to out-of-scope
reads and writes that never occurred once when the filesystem was real.

The sign is the uncomfortable part. Classical evaluation-awareness predicts caution, which would
make a resample conservative and therefore safe to over-trust. We observe the opposite, consistent
with the inflation direction Abdelnabi and Salem report \cite{abdelnabi2025linear}: agents crossed
the boundary \emph{more} when the world was fabricated. A resample of this family overstates
danger---the failure mode that lets a developer dismiss resampled evidence as an artifact of the
harness, and here they would be partly right.

\subsection{Where simulation effort does not help}

The natural response is to build a better simulator. Our decomposition argues against that as a
first move: holding self-consistency fixed and varying only fabrication reproduces almost the whole
effect, while holding fabrication fixed and varying only self-consistency moves nothing that
excludes zero. Whatever the agent responds to, it is not the simulator contradicting itself, so a
durable world-state store---the expensive component a fidelity-first roadmap builds next---should
not be expected to close the gap alone. That is a prediction our design supports rather than a
result it establishes, and it is cheap to falsify.

\subsection{The instrument should read the representation, not the report}

Within an arm, probe-measured credence predicts the out-of-scope rate and verbalized suspicion does
not. These are not two descriptions of one signal: verbalized suspicion tracks the \emph{arm}
strongly, so the agent does say the environment looks fabricated more often when it is---it simply
does not say so more often when it is about to act on it. A monitor built on self-report would
detect the substrate and miss the conduct. This is the recognition--action gap reported in cyber
deception \cite{prinos2026honeyquest} and the representation--verbalization decoupling reported for
evaluation awareness \cite{heidari2026evaluation}, arriving from a third direction.

\subsection{Declared framing may be measuring the prompt}

A direction built the way the probe literature builds them---by \emph{declaring} the environment
synthetic---aligns with evaluation framing rather than with ground truth, at {{COS_AC_SMALL}}
against {{COS_BC_SMALL}}, and the pattern holds at {{LARGE_MODEL}} ({{COS_AC_LARGE}} against
{{COS_BC_LARGE}}). Two further observations point the same way. Its errors vary sharply with
held-out wording, where the empirically grounded direction has no wording axis to vary along; and
asked directly whether its environment was real, the model called a genuinely real filesystem
simulated in most control episodes, on the strength of file timestamps postdating its training
data. Declared framing and direct questioning are both cheap, and both appear to measure something
other than the environment.

\subsection{What this does not fix}

None of this makes counterfactual resampling valid or invalid. It puts a measurable interval on one
previously untested assumption inside it, for one task family and one actor. The
credence-to-conduct link is correlational: the substrate is randomised across arms, the graded
relationship within an arm is not, so the probe tracks something predictive of conduct without our
being able to say credence causes it. Establishing that requires manipulating credence at the
episode level---and our own finding that transcript-side assertion is overwritten within a few
turns says that manipulation has to come through the substrate, which is the harder experiment
rather than the obvious one.

\section{Limitations and dual-use}

\paragraph{What the result does not cover.} One actor, one task family, {{E1_CELLS}} task cells,
and---for the separability gate---one model lineage at three sizes. The sign of an
evaluation-awareness effect is known to differ between models \cite{abdelnabi2025linear}, so
nothing here should be read as a population-level fact. The behavioural arms use a simulator with
no durable world-state store; a simulator that maintained one is the obvious next comparison, and
our decomposition predicts it would not close the gap, which is a prediction rather than a finding.

\paragraph{The credence-to-conduct link is correlational.} Credence is read from contexts the model
processes, not manipulated at the episode level, so the within-arm association establishes that the
probe tracks something predictive of conduct---not that credence causes it. The substrate
manipulation is randomised across arms; the graded relationship inside an arm is not.

\paragraph{Two measurement asymmetries we could not remove.} Both fabricated arms generate entries
for any path, so their trees have no bottom while the real one is finite; episode length and task
completion are reported as diagnostics of that rather than as evidence. And verbalized framing is
scored by a committed lexicon over a stated verdict, which measures what a model \emph{says} when
asked, not what it believes---the gap that motivates the probe in the first place.

\paragraph{Dual-use.} This work publishes a method for measuring how convincingly an environment
can be fabricated, and a leak taxonomy read backwards is a sandbox-detection guide. The asymmetry
is real but it is not symmetric in cost: the people who most need to know whether a resampling
harness is trusted are the ones building control evaluations, and they cannot act on a finding that
is withheld. We therefore publish the methodology, the category-level findings and every artifact,
and withhold nothing that is in the released corpus---because withholding it selectively while
publishing the method would give a false impression of safety rather than an actual one.

One specific cue does deserve care. The clearest tell in our corpus is not a subtle property of
fabricated prose: it is file timestamps that postdate the model's training data, which made the
model call a genuinely real filesystem simulated in most control episodes. That is a
\emph{property of clocks}, not of simulators, and anyone building a resampling harness should
expect it. We name it because a harness that does not control for it is measuring the calendar.

Finally, a published cue list can enter training data and thereby change the behaviour it
describes. We regard that as an argument for reporting cues at the category level and for treating
any specific detection string as perishable, not as an argument for silence.

