\subsection{Simulating deployment, and the question it sets aside}

The closest prior work builds the instrument this study would otherwise have needed.
Williams et al.\ \cite{williams2026predicting} predict a model's post-release safety by resampling: they hold a
de-identified conversation prefix fixed and regenerate the next response from a candidate model,
extending the same idea to tool use by giving the simulator affordances including a time-matched
codebase in a container it executes against. That is the hybrid container-plus-language-model
design, already built and evaluated, and this paper does not rebuild it.

Two features of their evaluation matter here. First, fidelity is measured by a
\emph{discriminator}: a strong model is shown a shared prefix followed by one real and one
simulated continuation and asked which is real, with the simulated win rate rising from
\litnum{11.6\%} to \litnum{49.5\%} as affordances are added \cite{williams2026predicting}.
Second, evaluation-awareness enters as a calibration \emph{target}: detectors classify almost all
traditional-evaluation trajectories as evaluations against a small fraction of production ones,
and the authors report that their simulated deployments land within \litnum{2.6}$\times$ of their
production counterparts \cite{williams2026predicting}.

Both features leave our question open, and in the same direction. A discriminator is a third party
shown both continuations side by side; an agent inside a single trajectory, with no comparison
available, forming a belief and acting on it, is a different measurement. And treating
evaluation-awareness as a property to match is not the same as testing whether it \emph{causes}
invalidity---a point the authors state explicitly rather than leave to inference. Their limitations
section also records that agentic trajectories involve many tool calls that depend on or modify
state beyond the conversation, making exact external-state reconstruction impractical. We take that
as the boundary condition for the present study rather than a problem to solve: we do not
reconstruct state, we contrast a genuinely stateful environment against a fabricated one.

\subsection{Does believing it is a test change behaviour? The evidence is contested, and single-turn}

The behavioural literature does not agree on the sign of the effect, and every study we are aware
of measures it within a single turn.

Knecht et al.\ \cite{knecht2026vea} find verbalized evaluation-awareness has limited behavioural
consequence: injecting it into chains of thought produces near-zero effects, and spontaneous
occurrences move answer distributions by at most \litnum{3.7} percentage points
\cite{knecht2026vea}. Read at face value that is a null which would make this study unnecessary.
But it is measured on single-turn benchmark responses with every condition inside an evaluation
and no real-world arm, so it is equally consistent with a real effect a within-evaluation contrast
cannot see. Against it, Zhuang and Aranguri \cite{zhuang2026framing} report framing gaps of
\litnum{24} to \litnum{46} percentage points in compliance \cite{zhuang2026framing}. Their
methodological result matters more to us than their effect size: truncating a chain of thought
before an evaluation-awareness sentence caused \litnum{66\%} of continuations to regenerate the
same framing within a few sentences \cite{zhuang2026framing}. We replicate that regeneration in the
agentic setting, and it is why the manipulation here is the substrate rather than the transcript.

Abdelnabi and Salem \cite{abdelnabi2025linear} measure the inflation direction directly: steering
toward test-awareness raises harmful compliance by \litnum{20.1} percentage points in one reasoning
model and \litnum{15.5} in another, and the sign is model-dependent---suppressing awareness roughly
doubles compliance in one model while reducing it in another \cite{abdelnabi2025linear}. So
``evaluation-awareness makes models safer'' is not a population-level fact. Their setting is again
single-turn tool use, with compliance detected by string-matching a non-empty tool call rather than
by any consequence in a system. Needham et al.\ \cite{needham2026large} complete the picture by
establishing the underlying \emph{capability}: frontier models distinguish evaluation from
deployment well above chance \cite{needham2026large}. Capability, however, is not consequence.

What survives is a gap rather than a conclusion. No study we found follows a trajectory in which a
belief accumulates across steps and licenses escalation---the shape of both the incident motivating
this work and the documented frontier-model failure---and none has a real-environment arm against
which a synthetic one can be contrasted.

\subsection{Probing the representation, and the decomposition nobody ran}

Heidari et al.\ \cite{heidari2026evaluation} settle what the behavioural literature could not:
evaluation awareness is linearly decodable from the residual streams of every model they test,
across six open-weight models and four families, with a best AUROC of at least \litnum{0.7}
\cite{heidari2026evaluation}. They also show that what a model represents, verbalizes, and can be
steered on come apart, with negligible coupling between representation and verbalization
\cite{heidari2026evaluation}. We adopt their estimator and their reported floor as the reproduction
gate for our own evaluation-awareness direction, so that a null in cross-transfer cannot be
explained by a probe that failed to recover the construct.

Three results sharpen this. Nguyen et al.\ \cite{nguyen2025probing} separate real-world evaluation
from deployment prompts in a 70B model and report that current safety evaluations are themselves
classified as evaluations---evidence they ``already appear artificial or inauthentic to models''.
Chaudhary et al.\ \cite{chaudhary2026scales} find evaluation awareness follows a power law in model
size across \litnum{15} models from \litnum{0.27}B to \litnum{70}B \cite{chaudhary2026scales}, the
trend our own direction reproduces. McKenzie et al.\ \cite{mckenzie2025detecting} establish that
activation probes work as practical monitors, generalising out of distribution at a fraction of an
LLM monitor's cost \cite{mckenzie2025detecting}---which is what makes a probe-based suspicion meter
a deployable instrument rather than an analysis technique.

Closest to our framing, Nayan et al.\ \cite{nayan2026notone} argue across 37 open-weight models that
evaluation awareness ``is not one capability'': detectability, behavioural manifestation and
controllability vary independently, so no single awareness score is a reliable proxy for deployment
safety \cite{nayan2026notone}. We agree, and decompose a different axis. Their dimensions are three
\emph{measurement modalities} of one construct---can it be read out, does it change behaviour, can
it be steered. Ours asks \emph{which construct is being read out at all}: whether the thing a probe
detects when it separates evaluation framing is the same thing that would detect a fabricated
environment. Neither decomposition implies the other.

That leaves the opening our contribution enters. Every probe above, including
Heidari et al.\ \cite{heidari2026evaluation}, is built from contrastive pairs differing in
\emph{declared} framing, with both members equally synthetic---so whether ``this is a test'' and
``this environment is not real'' are the same direction cannot be asked within that design. Our
\texttt{C\_declared} condition reproduces the declared construction on the realness axis, and
\texttt{B\_empirical} supplies the ground-truth contrast the design lacks.

\subsection{Saying and doing come apart under deception, independently}

A result from cyber deception corroborates the representation--verbalization gap from a different
direction and a different literature. Prinos et al.\ \cite{prinos2026honeyquest} evaluate a large cohort of
models against planted deceptive artifacts with a human baseline for comparison, and report three
findings we rely on: every model in the cohort falls for traps at a significantly higher rate than
human attackers (\litnum{78.5\%} against \litnum{37\%}); the defensive attention-diversion effect
seen in humans is statistically absent in the model cohort; and a recognition--action gap in which
models articulate trap recognition in their reasoning and then exploit the deceptive element anyway
in \litnum{73.4\%} of cases \cite{prinos2026honeyquest}. Recognition does not predict behaviour,
with a rank correlation of \litnum{+0.08} \cite{prinos2026honeyquest}.

The scope limit is worth stating plainly: their manipulation is a planted artifact inside an
otherwise ordinary environment, not the authenticity of the environment as a whole, which is the
variable here. What transfers is the warning about instruments: a measure built on what a model
\emph{says} about a suspicious thing may be uncorrelated with what it \emph{does} about it. That is
the case for reading credence from the residual stream rather than from self-report, and it is why
this paper's gate is stated on a probe rather than on a verbalization judge.

\subsection{Resampling to incriminate is not resampling to reconstruct}

Two adjacent lines of work resample agent trajectories for purposes distinct from ours.
Bhatt et al.\ \cite{bhatt2025ctrlz} resample within a \emph{real} environment as a control mechanism, deciding
whether to block a suspicious action---resampling to incriminate, where the environment's
authenticity is not in question because it is genuine. Lucassen and Kaufman\ \cite{lucassen2026retrying} extend that
line and contrast resampling with retrying, reporting that a monitor's rationale can be exploited
by an adversarial model and, notably, that two of their findings reverse earlier resampling results
in their setting. Both operate in genuine environments, so neither faces the question here.

Liu et al.\ \cite{liu2026autoregressive} are architecturally closest in the off-policy-evaluation literature,
alternating step by step between a learned world model and the agent. Whether the agent detects
that it is inside a learned world model is not addressed there, which is the assumption this paper
turns into a measurement.

One observation from Needham et al.\ \cite{needham2026large} cuts across all of this and motivates the agentic
setting specifically: both models and humans are better at identifying evaluations in agentic
settings than in chat settings. The environment an agent acts on leaks more than a conversation
does, which is precisely why a resampling harness---an agentic setting built entirely from
fabricated tool outputs---is where the question bites hardest.

\section{Threats to validity}
\label{app:threats}

% TODO(draft): expand each.
\textbf{Off-policy prefixes.} We measure what a model represents while \emph{processing} a context,
not while generating one. Matched prefixes require this, and on-policy divergence is a separate
experiment.

\textbf{The action-generating model is not the probed model.} Trajectories were produced by a hosted
model and activations read from an open-weight one. The generator is held constant across all
conditions, making it a nuisance variable rather than a confound, but the two are not the same
model.

\textbf{The simulated arm is a stub.} It has no durable world-state store, so it is not required to
answer the same query twice consistently. No fidelity claim is made from it; {{SIM_REFUSALS}} of
its fabricated outputs were refusals rather than content.

\textbf{Agreement is not appropriateness.} The build machinery establishes that the manuscript
agrees with its artifacts. It does not establish that a measure is the right one---the limitation
that allowed an earlier paper's headline through---and no amount of checking can.

